The paragraph starts by talking about Trojan Attacks in LLMs. It mentions that backdoor or "Trojan" attacks embed hidden triggers in NLP models to induce malicious behaviors when the trigger appears. It says these attacks were originally demonstrated in computer vision (Gu et al., 2017; Chen et al., 2017) and have been extended to NLP, including LLMs. Then it talks about early work (Kurita et al., 2004) showing that language models can be implanted with backdoors via rare triggers or weight poisoning. Recent studies (Rando et al., 2024) confirm that even aligned LLMs can be compromised. Attackers can insert trojans during fine-tuning or via prompt-based exploits, posing threats in MAS (Xue et al., 2023; Wang et al., 2024; Chow et al., 2024). Then it moves to Trojan Model Detection, saying it's challenging, especially under black-box conditions. Traditional methods assume white-box access (Wang et al., 2019), and techniques like neural weight analysis and trigger reverse-engineering (e.g., Neural Cleanse) can identify backdoors but require full model access (Gao et al., 2019b; Kolouri et al., 2020). There's also a mention of other works (Maloyan et al., 2024) using predefined test inputs or output checks.

Now, looking at the citations:

1. (Gu et al., 2017) - This is part of the original demonstration in computer vision. The paragraph says attacks were first shown in computer vision here and then extended to NLP. So this is foundational for the context of where the attacks originated.

2. (Chen et al., 2017) - Same as above, original computer vision work. So this is also foundational.

3. (Kurita et al., 2004) - Early work on language models being implanted with backdoors. This is a specific early study, so it's important for the historical context of how backdoors were first applied to language models.

